1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:07,312
One transfer. Three services. One question: can you prove that the money moved exactly

2
00:00:07,325 --> 00:00:07,875
once?

3
00:00:10,086 --> 00:00:15,711
Start with a precise prompt. Ask for an isolated fixture, stable locators, and

4
00:00:15,723 --> 00:00:20,923
assertions against real business state. Review the proposed code before you execute

5
00:00:20,936 --> 00:00:21,211
it.

6
00:00:23,432 --> 00:00:28,357
The code opens our local application. Every test owns a fresh source account and

7
00:00:28,370 --> 00:00:34,132
beneficiary. The test expresses a business promise, with an exact balance assertion.

8
00:00:36,346 --> 00:00:40,658
Watch the browser select the account, enter one hundred pesos, and submit the

9
00:00:40,671 --> 00:00:46,971
transfer. This is a recorded run against the local application. The form responds with

10
00:00:46,984 --> 00:00:51,234
a reference, and the balance changes from one thousand to nine hundred pesos.

11
00:00:53,460 --> 00:00:59,498
Now follow the identity. The gateway accepts the command. The accounts service commits

12
00:00:59,510 --> 00:01:04,335
the debit and publishes an event. The independent ledger projection catches up

13
00:01:04,348 --> 00:01:04,948
afterward.

14
00:01:07,166 --> 00:01:12,941
The balance is nine hundred pesos. One canonical record carries this exact reference.

15
00:01:13,804 --> 00:01:19,066
A visible success message alone is not enough. Verify both the identity and the

16
00:01:19,078 --> 00:01:19,679
effect.

17
00:01:21,904 --> 00:01:26,866
Use the same reference at the terminal boundary. TNZ enters it into the local

18
00:01:26,879 --> 00:01:32,279
simulator, waits for the result, and verifies the amount and balance. Playwright

19
00:01:32,292 --> 00:01:36,116
controls the browser. TNZ controls the terminal.

20
00:01:38,346 --> 00:01:43,796
Python and Java use the same business contract. Keep their fixture and lifecycle rules

21
00:01:43,809 --> 00:01:48,671
native to each runner. The same balance assertion must protect the same customer

22
00:01:48,684 --> 00:01:49,259
outcome.

23
00:01:51,476 --> 00:01:57,839
Now control a failure. This browser interception returns service unavailable. The form

24
00:01:57,851 --> 00:02:03,514
recovers and the account remains unchanged. That is a user interface experiment, not

25
00:02:03,526 --> 00:02:05,176
proof of a real backend outage.

26
00:02:07,390 --> 00:02:12,603
Run the suites and inspect the evidence. Twenty-two checks passed in this local run,

27
00:02:12,778 --> 00:02:18,752
across TypeScript, Python, Java, and the terminal protocol. Keep reports connected to

28
00:02:18,765 --> 00:02:20,728
the cases they actually executed.

29
00:02:22,944 --> 00:02:29,019
From prompt, to code, to browser, to terminal. Keep one identity, clear boundaries,

30
00:02:29,219 --> 00:02:33,906
and assertions that can catch a real defect. That is the workflow you will build in

31
00:02:33,919 --> 00:02:34,656
this course.